VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: W4
ATTEMPT: 2
---
Baseline (green, not decisive on its own):
- go build ./...  : exit 0
- go vet ./...    : exit 0
- go test ./...   : all packages ok (full suite, ~40 packages, incl. new
  internal/handlers/dashboard tests added for this attempt)
- make css-verify : "tailwind.css is up to date"

Attempt-1 defect: CONFIRMED FIXED for the case it was reported on.
kpis.html's per-bucket Living/Health rows (web/templates/components/kpis.html
lines 155-168) now render `.BudgetVerdict.Living.Status` /
`.BudgetVerdict.Healthcare.Status` — the same BucketFigure the banner
consumes — instead of a bare `gt/lt 0.0` comparison on the raw metrics
field. Reproduced the exact attempt-1 fixture (HealthcareCumulativeDelta =
0.60, CumulativeDelta = -3000, CombinedCumulativeDelta = -2999.40) via a
custom test driving BuildBudgetVerdict + RenderToString for both
"dashboard-verdict-bar" and "kpis": banner says "Healthcare: $0.60 on
target", card's Health row now also says "$0.60 on target" (gray), not "over"
(rose). Boundary sweep at 0.99/1.00/1.01 and -0.99/-1.00/-1.01 on each bucket
independently, banner vs card, all agree at every point (both use the
`onBudgetEps` = 1.0 inclusive boundary via the shared classifyBucket). The
project's own new test, TestBudgetKPICardAndVerdictBanner_AgreeWithinDeadBand,
and the byte-identity regression test for the HasTarget=false path, both
pass and are not vacuous — verified they fail against a manual revert of the
kpis.html classification to bare `gt .Metrics.CumulativeDelta 0.0`.

Other-figure attack surface: PerMonthDelta / HealthcarePerMonthDelta on the
Monthly Living Expenses / Monthly Healthcare KPI cards (kpis.html lines
73-74, 107-108) use a bare `gt/lt 0.0` with NO dead-band, same as before this
task. This is a genuinely different figure (per-month average vs. cumulative
total-period delta) rendered on different, distinctly-labeled cards ("$X/mo
over" vs. the Budget card's "over budget"/banner's "over N.N months"). Per
the task's own framing this is a different-figure case, not a same-figure
disagreement — noted, not failed. The MCP summarize_spending tool
(internal/services/mcpsvc/spend/summary.go) only echoes raw
living_monthly_delta/healthcare_monthly_delta/combined_cumulative_delta
numbers with no over/under/on-target verdict string at all, so it cannot
contradict the banner's classification — no defect there.

REAL DEFECT FOUND — phantom "on target" row for an unconfigured bucket,
exactly the failure mode the dispatch asked to rule out under point (4).

kpis.html's Living/Health rows (lines 155-168) are NOT gated on
`.Metrics.HasBudgetTarget` / `.Metrics.HasHealthcareTarget` (or on
`.BudgetVerdict.Living.Configured` / `.Healthcare.Configured`) the way the
banner's decomposition line is (dashboard-verdict-bar.html: `{{if
$v.Living.Configured}}...{{end}}` / `{{if $v.Healthcare.Configured}}...{{end}}`,
each independently gated). The two rows in kpis.html render unconditionally
whenever the *outer* `{{if .Metrics.HasCombinedTarget}}` block is entered —
and `HasCombinedTarget` is true whenever `budgetTarget + healthcareTarget >
0` (internal/services/metrics/metrics.go line ~244), i.e. it is true even
when only ONE of the two targets is configured. This is not a contrived
state: it is the ordinary case of a user who has set a living budget but no
healthcare target (or vice versa).

Reproduced with the real production code path (metrics.Calculate, not a
hand-built struct): budgetTarget=1000, healthcareTarget=0, one $500 grocery
transaction.
  m := metrics.Calculate(ts, start, end, 1000, 0)
  m.HasCombinedTarget == true, m.HasHealthcareTarget == false
  v := BuildBudgetVerdict(m); v.Healthcare.Configured == false

Banner output (correct): only "Living: $500.00... under" — no "Healthcare:"
line at all, per the banner's per-field `{{if ... .Configured}}` guards.

Budget KPI card output (same page, same data), unconditionally renders:
  <span>Health: <span class="num">$0.00</span> of <span class="num">$0.00</span></span>
  <span class="text-gray-500 ...">
      <span class="num">$0.00</span> on target
  </span>

The card fabricates a "Health: $0.00 of $0.00 — $0.00 on target" line for a
bucket that has no target at all, while the banner directly above correctly
omits any mention of healthcare. A user reading the page sees the card
assert healthcare spending is tracked and compliant ("on target") when in
fact nothing was ever budgeted for it — the same misleading-headroom concern
the whole task exists to close, just via omission-vs-fabrication instead of
via a numeric mismatch. This exactly matches the scenario the dispatch
explicitly asked to check under item (4) ("no phantom 'on target' row for an
unconfigured bucket") and it is present. The `classifyBucket`
unconfigured-bucket path is not itself buggy (it correctly forces
Status="on target"/Delta=0 rather than exposing a raw comparison over a
degenerate value) — the bug is that kpis.html doesn't gate the two card rows
on Configured at all, so an unconfigured bucket's forced-neutral BucketFigure
still gets a visible line instead of being suppressed like the banner
suppresses it.

This is inherited from kpis.html's pre-existing (pre-task) structure — the
Living/Health `<div>` rows were unconditional in master too — so it is not a
new regression introduced by this specific diff. But the task's own inline
comment added by this fix ("no bare >0/<0 comparison here, so the same delta
can never render 'on target' in one surface and 'over' in the other") and
the ruling's framing both claim the card is now fully consistent with the
banner, and the boss's dispatch explicitly asked this attempt to be checked
against precisely this case. It is not consistent: the banner and card
disagree about whether healthcare is being tracked at all for the identical
underlying DashboardMetrics.

Both defect-reproduction tests were written against the real templates/real
metrics.Calculate output (not a hand-authored struct asserting its own
premise) and fail on the current attempt-2 tree; boundary-sweep and the
attempt-1 exact-repro tests pass, isolating the failure to the
unconfigured-bucket path specifically.

Verdict: FAIL. The $0.60-dead-band contradiction from attempt 1 is fixed.
A new (or rather, previously-latent and now explicitly back in scope)
same-page contradiction survives for any user with only one budget bucket
configured: the banner omits the unconfigured bucket, the adjacent KPI card
fabricates an "on target" line for it.
